---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="about — tiny soft computer"
  description="The pedagogy and references behind the tiny soft computer workshop."
  active="about"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">about the workshop</p>
    <h1>what actually makes something a computer?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      most people assume a computer means a screen, a keyboard, a
      rectangle you stare at. this workshop starts by taking that
      apart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>a basic definition</h2>
    <p>any device that can:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>store instructions, a program</li>
      <li>execute those instructions in sequence</li>
      <li>process input, even if the only input is "power turns on", into output</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      by this definition, a thermostat, a calculator, a player piano,
      and your tiny soft computer all count. they vary in
      <em>generality</em>, not in whether they're really computers. a
      laptop is general-purpose, it can run different programs and do
      different things. your kit runs one small program, and was never
      meant to run anything else. that's not a lesser kind of computer.
      it's just a more honest one, one you can actually see all the way
      through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"><img class="divider-icon" src="/images/blossom-divider.png" alt=""></div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>references</h2>
    <p>two pieces that shaped how we think about this project.</p>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>the embroidered computer</h3>
      <p class="credit">irene posch + ebru kurbak, part of the stitching worlds project</p>
      <p>
        a working 8-bit electromechanical computer made entirely from
        gold, linen, hematite, wood, silver, and copper thread. it
        functions as a computer and as a decorative textile at the same
        time, using embroidered relays the way pre-semiconductor
        computers once did. no conventional electronic components at
        all.
      </p>
      <p>
        it also carries a real historical thread worth knowing: ada
        lovelace once observed that the analytical engine "weaves
        algebraic patterns, just as the jacquard loom weaves flowers
        and leaves." the jacquard loom, with its binary punch-card
        storage, is widely considered a predecessor to the modern
        computer. textiles and computing share real ancestry, this
        workshop continues that lineage, it doesn't invent it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>1-bit symphony</h3>
      <p class="credit">tristan perich</p>
      <p>
        a cd jewel case with a microchip and speaker wired directly onto
        the sleeve, wiring exposed, nothing hidden. the chip is
        programmed with a full 40-minute symphony, compressed onto a
        single 8kb chip, performed live in real time every time it's
        switched on. it's not a recording. it's computation, stripped
        down to almost nothing, with the workings left in plain sight.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      one soft and hidden, one bare and exposed, both are legitimate
      answers to the same question. your enclosure doesn't have to look
      like either. this is one answer, what's yours?
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"><img class="divider-icon" src="/images/blossom-divider.png" alt=""></div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>a limit we chose to keep</h2>
    <p>
      the display in your kit can only redraw itself once every 180
      seconds (3 minutes), exactly what the manufacturer's own datasheet specifies for
      this exact panel, tied to how the ink itself physically moves. that's
      not a shortcut we didn't have time to fix. we could have forced it
      faster in code. we chose not to.
    </p>
    <p>
      a computer that makes you wait is unfashionable. but waiting isn't
      always a failure of design, sometimes it's the most honest thing
      a piece of technology can ask of you. this workshop is as much
      about the moments where you work with a material's real limits as
      it is about the electronics themselves.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"><img class="divider-icon" src="/images/blossom-divider.png" alt=""></div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>the facilitators</h2>
    <p>
      led by <a href="https://instagram.com/alannabean">Alanna Okun</a>
      and <a href="https://instagram.com/electrocutelab">Yafira Martinez</a>
      (Electrocute Lab), drawing on their combined backgrounds in making
      and teaching. the workshop covers both the build and the pedagogy
      behind it, how the kit was designed to be beginner-friendly, and
      how to pass that on to others.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>